2025-08-30 15:48:36 [simon@midnightblue] support scp file copy with sloppynet?
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat] To add SCP support to sloppynet, you'll need to handle the `sftp` subsystem in your SSH server.
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat] Here's the minimal modification:
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat] Add this inside your `sshClient.on("ready")` handler, after the session handling:
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat] sshClient.on("sftp", (accept, reject) => {
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]   const sftp = accept();
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]   sftp.on("OPEN", (reqId, filename, flags, attrs) => {
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]     // Handle file open requests
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]     sftp.status(reqId, STATUS_CODE.FAILURE);
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]   });
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]   sftp.on("READ", (reqId, handle, offset, length) => {
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]     // Handle file read requests
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]     sftp.status(reqId, STATUS_CODE.FAILURE);
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]   });
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]   sftp.on("WRITE", (reqId, handle, offset, data) => {
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]     // Handle file write requests
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]     sftp.status(reqId, STATUS_CODE.FAILURE);
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]   });
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat]   // Add other SFTP handlers as needed
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat] });
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat] You'll also need to import SFTP constants:
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat] import { STATUS_CODE } from "npm:ssh2";
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat] This provides basic SCP/SFTP support - you'd need to implement the actual file operations for full
2025-08-30 15:48:52 [deepseek-chat] functionality.
2025-08-30 15:49:33 [simon@midnightblue] is there an rfc?
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] Yes, SCP operates over the SFTP protocol, which is standardized in:
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] • RFC 913* - Simple File Transfer Protocol (obsolete)
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] • RFC 959* - File Transfer Protocol (FTP)
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] • RFC 4251* - SSH Protocol Architecture
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] • RFC 4253* - SSH Transport Layer Protocol
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] • RFC 4254* - SSH Connection Protocol
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] Most importantly for SFTP/SCP:
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] • RFC 4250* - SSH Assigned Numbers (includes SFTP packet types)
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] • Draft-ietf-secsh-filexfer* - SSH File Transfer Protocol (the de facto SFTP standard)
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] SCP itself uses the SSH connection protocol (RFC 4254) and typically runs over the SFTP subsystem,
2025-08-30 15:49:46 [deepseek-chat] though some implementations use direct exec channels with `scp` command.
2025-08-31 14:22:44 [simon@midnightblue] hi gemini
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] Based on the provided `sloppynet.ts` code, SCP file copy is not currently supported. The server
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] handles shell sessions, but lacks the necessary `exec` channel or `sftp` subsystem implementation
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] that SCP clients typically use for file transfers.
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] Regarding an RFC:
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] SCP (Secure Copy Protocol) itself is not defined by a specific RFC in the same way SSH or SFTP are.
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] It is a protocol that leverages the SSH `exec` channel to run a remote `scp` command (e.g., `scp
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] -t` for "to" or `scp -f` for "from") and transfer data over the standard input/output of that
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] command.
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] SFTP (SSH File Transfer Protocol), which is a more structured file transfer protocol often
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] preferred over SCP, is defined by an RFC, specifically RFC 4251 and related documents, as a
2025-08-31 14:22:51 [gemini-2.5-flash] subsystem of SSH.
2025-08-31 14:23:52 [simon@midnightblue] how much code to support receiving files sent with scp
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] Yes, it is possible to support SCP file copy with `sloppynet.ts` using the `ssh2` library, but it
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] requires implementing the SCP protocol yourself on the server side.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] • Is there an RFC?*
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] SCP itself is not defined by a formal RFC in the same way SSH (which it relies upon) is. SSH is
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] defined by several RFCs (e.g., RFC 4251 for the architecture, RFC 4252 for authentication, RFC 4253
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] for the transport layer, and RFC 4254 for the connection protocol).
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] The SCP protocol is more of a de-facto standard based on the behavior of the original `rcp` (remote
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] copy) utility over an SSH channel. You can find unofficial specifications and descriptions of the
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] protocol, often referenced from OpenSSH documentation or the `rcp` man page. It's essentially a set
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] of commands and acknowledgments sent over an SSH `exec` channel.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] • How much code to support receiving files sent with SCP?*
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] Implementing SCP support, even just for receiving files, is a non-trivial amount of code and
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] effort. It's not a single function call. You would need to:
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] 1.  Listen for `exec` requests: The `ssh2` `Session` object in your `onSSHConnection`'s
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] `session` handler needs to listen for the `'exec'` event. When an SCP client initiates a transfer,
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] it executes a command like `scp -t path/to/destination` on the server.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     // Inside session.on("shell", ...) or a new exec handler
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     session.on("exec", (accept, reject, info) => {
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]         const command = info.command;
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]         // Check if the command starts with 'scp -t' for receiving or 'scp -f' for sending
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]         if (command.startsWith('scp -t')) {
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]             const stream = accept(); // Accept the exec channel
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]             // Implement SCP receive logic here
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]             handleScpReceive(stream, command, connection.name); // Pass stream, command, and context
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]         } else if (command.startsWith('scp -f')) {
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]             // ... handle sending if desired ...
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]             reject(); // Or reject if only receiving is supported
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]         } else {
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]             reject(); // Command not recognized or not supported
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]         }
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     });
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] 2.  Implement the SCP Receive Protocol (`handleScpReceive` function): This is where the
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] bulk of the code goes.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Acknowledge the command: The SCP server must send a null byte (`\x00`) back to the
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] client to acknowledge the `scp -t` command.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Parse incoming commands: The client will then send commands like:
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]         *   `C<permissions> <size> <filename>\n`: To indicate an incoming file. You'll need to
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] parse permissions, size, and filename.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]         *   `D<permissions> <mtime> <atime> <dirname>\n`: To indicate an incoming directory (if
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] `-r` is used).
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]         *   `E\n`: To indicate the end of a directory (if `-r` is used).
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   File/Directory Creation: Create the specified directories and files on your
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] server's filesystem. You'll need to handle path sanitization to prevent directory traversal
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] attacks.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Read File Data: When a `C` command is received, you read `size` bytes from the
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] `stream` and write them to the corresponding file.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Send Acknowledgments: After receiving a command, creating a file, or successfully
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] writing file data, you must send another null byte (`\x00`) back to the client. If an error occurs,
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] you send `\x01` followed by an error message, then `\x0a` (newline).
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Handle End of Transfer: The client will send a final `\x00` after all
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] files/directories are transferred.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] • Estimated Lines of Code:*
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] For a basic implementation to receive a single file (without handling directories,
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] recursion, or advanced error messages beyond simple failures), you would likely be looking at:
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] • ~10-20 lines to add the `session.on('exec')` handler.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] • ~100-200 lines for the `handleScpReceive` function, which would include:
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   State management (what stage of transfer are we in?).
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Parsing the incoming commands.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   File I/O (using `node:fs` or `Deno.writeFile`, `Deno.mkdir`, etc.).
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Sending acknowledgments and basic error responses.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Reading the file data in chunks.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] For a robust implementation that handles directories, recursion (`-r`), proper permissions, and
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] detailed error reporting, it would easily grow to:
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] • ~300-500+ lines, potentially split into a dedicated module or class. This would involve more
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] complex state machines, recursive functions for directory traversal, and careful handling of all
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] protocol nuances.
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] In summary, while the `ssh2` library provides the necessary low-level access, implementing the SCP
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] protocol on top of it for receiving files is a significant development task requiring careful
2025-08-31 14:24:08 [gemini-2.5-flash] attention to protocol details, file system interaction, and security.
2025-08-31 14:25:19 [simon@midnightblue] third party deno libs?
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] No problem, I'll keep your comments in mind.
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] 1.  support scp file copy with sloppynet?
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     The current `sloppynet.ts` code does not explicitly implement support for SCP file copying. It
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] focuses on shell sessions and command execution.
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] 2.  is there an rfc?
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     SCP itself isn't defined by a specific RFC. It typically operates by executing the `scp`
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] command remotely over an SSH "exec" channel, then handling data transfer via standard input/output
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] streams. The underlying SSH protocol is defined by RFCs (e.g., RFC 4251, 4252, 4253, 4254
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] for SSHv2). SFTP, a more structured file transfer protocol often used with SSH, does have
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] an RFC (RFC 4252 defines the SFTP protocol's architecture and message formats).
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] 3.  hi gemini
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     Hello!
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] 4.  how much code to support receiving files sent with scp
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     To support receiving files via SCP (using `scp -t filename`), you would need to add an `exec`
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] channel handler to your SSH server. This handler would:
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Detect commands starting with `scp -t`.
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Parse the filename and permissions from the command string.
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Create or open the target file on the server.
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Read incoming data from the channel's `stdin` (the client's data stream) and write it to
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] the file.
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     *   Send acknowledgements (null bytes for success) or error messages (non-null byte followed by
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] an error string) back to the client via `stdout`.
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     This would likely involve a new function or a dedicated block within the `session.on("exec")`
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] handler (which is not currently present in your code for general commands), and could range from
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] tens to a hundred or so lines of code for a basic, functional implementation, depending on error
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] handling and feature completeness (e.g., handling directories, recursive copies, permissions,
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] multiple files).
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] 5.  third party deno libs?
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash]     The primary third-party library used in your code is `npm:ssh2`, which is a Node.js package
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] being run in Deno's Node.js compatibility mode. `node:fs` is a Deno-native implementation of
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] Node.js's `fs` module, so while it provides Node.js compatibility, it's not a traditional
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] "third-party Deno library" in the sense of being a separate Deno-specific module published to a
2025-08-31 14:25:29 [gemini-2.5-flash] Deno registry.
